  "body": "Fork synced at v0.18.0 (branch cut from upstream/main 3e9b8ae1; upstream/main a6bd69d8, with #1032, merged in at 6ccef5e4). **Stacked on #1007** (`issue/983-rings-fallback-note`, merged into this branch at 1eff2424: it gives the rotary level reader the ladder's step on the same line of the r6.2 pin this PR rewrites). Until #1007 merges, its commits show in this diff; this PR's own change is commit 42ee5e38 (plus the decision note f23ebc3c).\n\n## What changed\n\nThe microlift acceptance (`adaptive/lift.rs`, `lift_returns`) compared the lifted plan's worst reading with the spiral's and flipped on nanometres. It was not a rounding tie: readings are whole rim samples (half degrees), and the extra sample was real. A piece re-thinned to longer chords (0.01 mm sag) cuts inside the arc it replaces and leaves a sliver on the stock side; the next piece meets it as one or two samples the spiral never planned, and which planner cells the sliver covers turns on nanometres of ring geometry.\n\n- `lift_coarse::cut` (new module, `coarsen` moved into it): a piece takes its longer chords only where they read within the bound **and** leave standing no cell its own chords clear (`Raster::stands_over`). Otherwise it is cut on its own chords, the spiral's.\n- `lift::keeps`: the acceptance keeps its law, with **no tolerance**. A lifted plan that reads over the spiral (or over the bound, where the spiral read under it) is never kept. The stated tolerance is zero.\n- No other planner file is touched (`grow.rs`, PR #953's, and `budget.rs` are as they were).\n\nWhy not the tolerance the issue asked for: a one-sample tolerance posts a plan that reads over the stated bound, and two samples are needed (48.5 was read). The decision file has the alternatives: `docs/decisions/982-microlift-acceptance.md`.\n\n## Readings (release, Linux x86-64)\n\n60 x 14 slot, D10, bound 47.5014 deg; lifted / spiral reading. \"Opening\" is `rings::open` eroding that much deeper than it dilates back, \"width\" the slot that much wider.\n\n| twin | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| unperturbed | 47.5 / 47.5 kept | 47.5 / 47.5 kept |\n| opening 8, 32, 48 nm | 48.0 / 47.5 **dropped** | 47.5 / 47.5 kept |\n| opening 180 nm | 48.5 / 47.5 **dropped** | 47.5 / 47.5 kept |\n| width -125, -62, -8, +32, +510 nm | 48.0 / 47.5 **dropped** | 47.5 / 47.5 kept |\n| width +8 nm | 48.5 / 47.5 **dropped** | 47.5 / 47.5 kept |\n| the other 19 twins (opening 16 nm - 1 um, width -250 nm - +5 um) | kept | kept |\n\nBefore, 10 of 30 twins dropped the lifted plan. After, 0 of 30, each reading its worst where the spiral does (the first turn out of the entry bore).\n\nCost: the `adaptive_hsm` narrow slot posts 14 534 moves instead of 13 378 (+8.6 %; a third of its pieces keep longer chords instead of two thirds); cut and air lengths within 3 mm; load, breaks and verdict unchanged. Planning time unchanged.\n\n## The one fixture verdict that changes\n\nThe issue's Done-means asks that no adaptive fixture's verdict change. One does, and has to: `rotary_adaptive`'s r6.2 neck levels (5.7, 5.2), pinned \"refused for travel\" since #55. That verdict was this same coin. On unchanged main, openings perturbed only:\n\n| opening | radius 5.7 | radius 5.2 | old pin |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0, 8, 125 nm | rings | rings | green |\n| 16, 62 nm | **HSM-planned** | rings | red |\n| 250, 510 nm | rings | **HSM-planned** | red |\n\nAfter the fix both levels read 47.5 / 47.5 and are HSM-planned on microlift returns at every perturbation tried (0, 16, 32, 250 nm). The pin is rewritten: `the_r62_neck_levels_are_hsm_planned_on_microlift_returns`. They were cut by Offset rings at 100 % D with 15-21 breaks of 45-135 deg; they now read 20.3 % D on the suite's 0.1 mm dexel, no planner overload. Two readings are pinned as read, not at the whole bar's laws, and appear on main too whenever the coin lands the other way: 20.3 % D at the first turn out of the entry bore (law 19.8 %), and three breaks of 5.1-5.5 deg on radius 5.2. Filed as #1050.\n\nEvery other verdict is unchanged: bore, rectangle, L-pocket, island, narrow slot, channel (`adaptive_hsm`), the band (`adaptive_band`), `pocket3d_hsm`, `level_links`, the other `rotary_adaptive` tests.\n\n## Goldens\n\nNone moves. `tests/golden/` holds no HSM plan, and no digest pins a lifted plan. Posted programs of lifted plans do change (more pieces on their own chords).\n\n## How tested\n\nPinning tests (all fail without the sliver check; the first two were run that way):\n- `adaptive::tests::the_slot_and_its_32_nm_twin_decide_the_same_way` (default lane)\n- `adaptive::lift_tests::a_piece_takes_longer_chords_only_where_they_leave_no_sliver` (default lane)\n- `adaptive::lift_tests::the_lifted_plan_is_kept_only_at_or_under_the_spiral_reading` (default lane; the zero tolerance)\n- `adaptive::tests::the_slot_keeps_its_verdict_under_sub_micron_perturbations` (heavy lane: 20 twins, 8 nm - 0.51 um)\n- `rotary_adaptive::the_r62_neck_levels_are_hsm_planned_on_microlift_returns` (heavy lane, rewritten)\n\nGate, on the final head 6ccef5e4: `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main` green, 0 failed, wall 4.7 min (every motion suite ran: the stack touches a shared test fixture). `cargo clippy -p camcraft-motion --all-targets -- -D warnings` clean.\n\nRelease `--include-ignored` (heavy lanes): motion lib 198/198 (the 20-twin sweep included), `adaptive_hsm` 6/6, `adaptive_band` 2/2, `rotary_adaptive` 6/6, `pocket3d_hsm` 4/4, `level_links` 12/12, `adaptive_orbit_seam` 1/1 (on 6ccef5e4). App (release, `--include-ignored`, on 42ee5e38): `roughing2d` 7/7, `shoulder_adaptive` 3/3, `pocket_overhead` 2/2, `whole_model_pocket` 5/5, `stock_engine_agreement` 3/3.\n\n## What was not exercised\n\n- **Windows and macOS.** Everything above is Linux x86-64. The planner's output differs in the last bits between platforms (#1010), so which twins flipped before differs there (the first worker saw 32 nm flip on Apple silicon); the fix does not depend on which.\n- **The app's STEP bore count pin** (`pocket3d_hsm::the_users_bore_roughs_adaptive_level_by_level_and_verifies_clean`, re-pinned per platform by #1032): on Linux it reads (16196, 15457, 1570) with and without this change, inside #1032's band for unmeasured platforms, so the exact Windows and macOS triples are not expected to move - but they were not measured. (Before #1032 it read (6490, 6454, 222) on Linux both ways.) A reviewer on Windows or macOS: the test prints its triple.\n- The rotary suite under perturbation beyond the r6.2 pin: the r7 bar's and the window's deepest levels flip on the #850 planning budget (main and this branch alike; the r7 pin is red with the openings 250 nm deeper on both). Filed as #1051.\n- #882's held speedup was not re-measured on top of this.\n- Debug-profile runs of the heavy lanes; UI; batch tier.\n\nChangelog: Adaptive: a microlifted piece is posted on longer chords only where they leave no sliver of stock, so the lifted plan no longer flips to the bare spiral (and the pocket to Offset rings) on nanometre changes of the geometry; the rotary r6.2 neck levels are HSM-planned on microlift returns instead of ring-cut.\n\nEffort: max (no escalation)\n\nFixes #982\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
